<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder, single episode, severe, without psychotic features (F32.2)</content>. PHQ-9 14, 30 percent below baseline, and GAD-7 7, three weeks into sertraline and CBT: early improvement, not yet a response. Suspicious ideas have resolved. No side effects; no suicidal thoughts or thoughts of harming others. [S]<list><item>Continue sertraline 50 mg; review in three weeks.</item><item>If the PHQ-9 has not fallen by at least 50 percent after six to eight weeks, increase to 100 mg.</item><item>Continue weekly CBT and collaborative-care review.</item><item>Safety plan unchanged; 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline confirmed.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Raised urine mercury from occupational exposure (R82.6)</content>. Urine mercury 31 ug/L (27 ug/g creatinine), down from 46 ug/L a month after removal, as expected with a urinary half-life of one to two months; blood mercury 3.1 ug/L, now normal. No signs of toxicity. [S]<list><item>Repeat 24-hour urine mercury, creatinine, and urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio at three months.</item><item>No return to the laboratory until it is re-measured and the fume hood is fitted.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic insomnia comorbid with depression (G47.00)</content>. About five and a half hours; sleep efficiency improved on the diary. [S]<list><item>Widen the sleep window by 15 minutes.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9</td><td>Baseline, then at least monthly during acute treatment; every 2 to 4 weeks in continuation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>14, down from 20 (30 percent); item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>GAD-7</td><td>With the PHQ-9 when anxiety accompanies depression</td><td>Completed today</td><td>7, down from 11</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide and violence risk reassessment (C-SSRS Since Last Visit, PHQ-9 item 9, and thoughts of harming others)</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No suicidal thoughts, intent, plan, or behavior; no thoughts of harming others; PHQ-9 item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>SSRI tolerability check two weeks after starting</td><td>2 weeks after starting and after each dose change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Done at the preventive visit two weeks after the start (no side effects) and again today</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adherence and adverse-effect review</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No missed doses; early nausea resolved; no bleeding, restlessness, or sexual side effects</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>24-hour urine mercury with creatinine correction, and blood mercury</td><td>At the exposure assessment, then 4 to 6 weeks after removal and at 3 months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Urine 31 ug/L and 27 ug/g creatinine, falling; blood 3.1 ug/L, normal; next at three months</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Kidney function and urine protein</td><td>At the exposure assessment and at 3 months</td><td>Due</td><td>Normal at intake; due at three months</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Signs of mercury toxicity: tremor (postural and intention), handwriting, gums and mouth, erethism, peripheral neuropathy</td><td>At the exposure assessment and at each follow-up while levels are raised</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No tremor, gingivitis, or neuropathy</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Workplace hazard control: re-measurement of mercury vapor after decontamination</td><td>After decontamination and before any return to the laboratory</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Missed: the safety office planned to re-measure the laboratory within two weeks of cleaning, but has not yet done so. No harm: the laboratory has stayed closed, he has had no exposure, and his urine mercury is falling. Corrective action: the clinic wrote to the safety office asking for the measurement and the fume-hood installation date, and his return to laboratory work stays on hold until both are confirmed</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Chelation and provoked (chelator-challenge) urine testing</td><td>Only for symptomatic patients with high levels, decided with a medical toxicologist</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Urine mercury below 100 ug/L with no tremor, gingivitis, or kidney injury; removal from exposure comes first, and provoked testing is not valid</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight and nutrition</td><td>Every visit while weight is below his usual</td><td>Completed today</td><td>64.8 kg, up 1.0 kg since the diagnosis visit; three meals a day</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Psychotherapy, sleep, and activity</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Three CBT sessions; sleep window widened; teaching fully; walks most days</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lithium, anticonvulsant, or antipsychotic monitoring (levels, metabolic tests, AIMS)</td><td>Only on those drugs</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Treated with an SSRI only; no antipsychotic, because there are no psychotic features</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Toxic and other medical causes assessed by the second visit. Outcome by 1693-10-08: Blood count, metabolic panel, TSH, vitamin B12, kidney tests, blood lead, and MoCA normal; blood and urine mercury raised from his work, below levels linked to overt toxicity [S]</item><item>No further exposure to mercury and other metals until the laboratory is made safe. In progress; target 1694-01-31 [S]</item><item>Response (PHQ-9 down by at least 50 percent) by eight to twelve weeks of treatment, then remission (PHQ-9 below 5). In progress; target 1693-12-31 [S]</item><item>No suicidal thoughts and no thoughts of harming others. In progress; target 1693-12-31 [S]</item><item>At least six hours of sleep most nights without hypnotics. In progress; target 1693-12-31 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026); APA Clinical Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Depression Across Three Age Cohorts (2019); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Major Depressive Disorder (2022); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); Management of Chronic Insomnia Disorder in Adults: A Clinical Practice Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2016); Evaluating Mercury Exposure: Information for Health Care Providers (2018); NIOSH Pocket Guide to Chemical Hazards: Mercury compounds [except (organo) alkyls] (as Hg) (2026); American College of Medical Toxicology Position Statement on Post-Chelator Challenge Urinary Metal Testing (2010); Use and Misuse of Metal Chelation Therapy (McKay CA, editorial) and Recommendations for Provoked Challenge Urine Testing (Ruha AM), proceedings of the ACMT symposium (2013)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Depression review in three weeks; urine mercury and kidney tests at three months; second zoster dose in two to six months. [S]</paragraph></text>

